Devcon 8 Proposal

Session title: Setting the bar on Ethereum wallets

Session type: Workshop (50 minutes)

Track: Security

Abstract: 
A hands-on workshop that breaks down what makes an Ethereum wallet “good”, and how standards shape interoperability across the ecosystem.
Participants will evaluate real wallets across key categories including transaction simulations, signatures, scam alerts, and EIP support: highlighting how these features impact UX.
We will also dive into recent EIPs like EIP-7730 (structured data signing) and EIP-8213 (calldata digest), and how they aim to improve wallet UX, safety, and standardization.

Additional Speakers: Polymutex / Matt

Tags: Wallets, UX, Security

The session assumes: No prior knowledge

How does your session relate to CROPS?:  
Ethereum is CROPS: Censorship-Resistant, Open, Private, and Secure. Walletbeat ensures users actually harness these values.
The Ethereum ecosystem has gone to painstaking lengths to ensure Ethereum-the-protocol embodies these values. However, this only matters if users actually get to harness these values.
For that to happen, every layer of the stack needs to be crafted with them in mind. Other layers are well-served by our peers at L2BEAT, Anticapture, DeFiScan, and others.
Walletbeat focuses on the last-mile wallet layer of the stack: users' gateway to Ethereum.
We ensure Ethereum values make it all the way to users.

